    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d103">

                <pb facs="0192" n="106" sameAs="frus:frus1927v01/pg106"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15 a 1/428: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Houghton</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-07-14T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 14, 1927—5
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>163. British Ambassador called on me this morning and read telegram containing
                    same suggestion quoted in your telegram No. 162, July 12, noon. Ambassador is
                    equally at loss to know precisely what Chamberlain means by “class”, and whole
                    confusion seems to arise on that point. I think it would be best, nevertheless,
                    if you would give Chamberlain an answer to his suggestion in which you would
                    make clear how his words are understood here and by our delegation at
                    Conference. This Government’s original proposal was clear and <pb facs="0193"
                        n="107" xml:id="pg107"/> definite and was not subject to misunderstanding,
                    and we should be especially careful that no confusion be permitted to arise from
                    use of same terms in different senses by the American and the British
                    Governments.</p>
                <p>In regard to adjournment of Conference, I did not intend in my telegram to you to
                    overrule our delegation’s decision, but merely to suggest to Gibson and his
                    colleagues that we thought that if they came to an impasse or if the British
                    Government wished to consult with Bridgeman, then an adjournment might serve a
                    useful purpose; certainly if British Government wanted an adjournment, we should
                    not object. At present Gibson thinks progress is being made, so there is no
                    immediate necessity for adjournment.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
